        <div class="entry-header">
          <h2 class="entry-title">Yes, I’m Taking This Personally.</h2>
          <p class="entry-meta">category: music/very much not music?!??!?!</p>
          <p class="entry-meta">date: 16th November 2026, Morning.</p>
          <p class="entry-intro">tl;dr: fuck ai, and especially fuck AI music. argue with the fucking wall.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="qa-block">
          <h3 class="qa-question">beta.</h3>
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>This is going to be a long post, and it probably won’t be super polished. I have a lot to say about this, so I’m just spilling my thoughts. In this post I am talking primarily here about my personal experience with AI, which doesn’t even begin to touch how it is affecting others. Just putting that out there.</p>
            <p>Now:</p>
            <p>It is 2022, and I’m making an account for this new website called OpenAI (This was the prototype before it was even <i>called</i> ChatGPT). After a few basic prompts like “hi,” the first thing I remember testing is if the ‘chatbot’ would write Breaking Bad fanfiction because I thought that would be funny. I think it did? I wonder if I have any screenshots of that. Hold on.</p>
          </div>
          <img class="entry-media framed" src="../blogimg/17/breakingbad.jpg" alt="Breaking Bad fanfiction in the OpenAI playground" loading="lazy">
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>I’m also asking it questions such as this one:</p>
          </div>
          <img class="entry-media framed" src="../blogimg/17/destiny.jpg" alt="OpenAI playground answering “I’m a destiny 2 player, will I find love?”" loading="lazy">
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>It was mesmerizing and entertaining how good this shit was at taking my words and exchanging them for generated responses. I played with this thing for a while, and had some laughs with it.</p>
            <p>Eventually, beta.openai.com stops even existing as a link altogether. It’s called ‘ChatGPT,’ and it does this now:</p>
          </div>
          <img class="entry-media framed" src="../blogimg/17/dougie.jpg" alt="ChatGPT error after the prompt “can you dougie”" loading="lazy">
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>fascinating.</p>
            <p>There’s a website called Craiyon, where you can make photos with a sentence, and my friends and I are doing funny stuff like this!</p>
          </div>
          <img class="entry-media framed" src="../blogimg/17/fortnite.jpg" alt="Craiyon results for “Hindenburg Disaster in Fortnite”" loading="lazy">
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>It’s not THAT visually convincing but it doesn’t have to be, It’s hilarious.</p>
            <p>It’s 2025, and this shit’s getting really good. It helps with homework I don’t want to do, and writing business emails and company outreach I could care less about. I’m using it in my coding utility, which actively contributes towards the cognitive atrophy I’m still wrestling with restoring. Pandora’s box is open.</p>
            <p>AI is everywhere. It’s in&hellip;</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-answer seq">
            <p>my desktop</p>
            <p>my speaker</p>
            <p>my first post-grad job</p>
            <p>my coding utilities</p>
            <p>my phone</p>
            <p>your phone</p>
            <p>QUIZLET?!</p>
            <p>fucking air traffic control</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>The advertising world has ditched its usual color palette for a “sleek” greyscale “do X with AI! Or do Y with AI!” It’s in my Google Search, my tiktok feed, and everywhere for that matter.</p>
            <p>Then, it’s 2026, last Friday to be specific. I’m in a car with some of my closest people, and we’re jamming to a song somebody queued.</p>
            <p>It’s a good song.</p>
            <p>I add it to my liked songs, jamming out, and halfway through, they reveal the song is AI generated. I had no idea. With absolutely ZERO hesitation, in a non-dramatic way, I un-like and skip the song, much to their dismay. They like the song and want to listen to it. They try to skip back twice. How could somebody who <i>knows</i> how much time and care I devote into songmaking put something like this on in front of me, and especially try to do it without me knowing what it was?</p>
            <p>For a good portion of the night, a conversation ensues about how this is the future.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-answer seq">
            <p>I hear about how the AI ‘artist’ has a person behind it directing it, so it’s still real music coming from a person. They have the song generated and they just tell it what to change around until it sounds like what they wanted.</p>
            <p>I hear about how AI generated songs are <i>just like</i> the synthesizer; they shook up the music world and I just have to ‘adapt!’</p>
            <p>I hear about how we’re expanding music creation to everybody now, not just people who know how to sing and play.</p>
            <p>I hear about how this only makes human musicians more valuable for ‘the people that want that.’</p>
            <p>I hear about how I shouldn’t be bothered by layers of computational theory, theory I studied in school, are now doing <i>everything</i> I could and then some. Writing and recording a song each day for 300 days isn’t that impressive if a program could generate hundreds, right?</p>
            <p class="punch">I was told it was strange that I seemed to be taking it personally.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="qa-block">
          <h3 class="qa-question">Suno, Spotify and so on:</h3>
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>Now it is last night. It’s 1AM, and I can’t sleep. I see some shit on my Instagram story about how Spotify’s got this historic deal with several major record labels that’ll allow users to take existing music and ‘remix it’ using AI:</p>
          </div>
          <img class="entry-media framed" src="../blogimg/17/spotify.png" alt="Details of Spotify’s AI remix feature and label licensing deals" loading="lazy">
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>I don’t need to wait for Laufey to cover a jazz standard, I can just have Spotify do it for me! And I can make it sound EXACTLY how I want it! You can have Billy Joel cover Whiskey Glasses, or Kanye West’s ‘runaway’ sound like a punk rocker song.</p>
            <p>There’ll be no quirks, no faults (unless I want them), and I won’t be able to tell the difference when my friend puts it on in the car.</p>
            <p>Sure, this is already happening. I heard a Juice Wrld song on Youtube that wasn’t him, and that shit was pretty convincing, but I think there’s a certain message that’s being sent by putting this on Spotify, One of the biggest digital gateways to music there is, and it being approved by major labels, probably without the consent of any of the artists they manage.</p>
            <p>Historically, Spotify doesn’t treat artists well. Platforms in general don’t. But this is actively putting artists second, and signaling that AI theft of their work is now just as valuable as the art they create. Their blood, their tears, heartbreaks and triumphs and human experience.</p>
            <p>They can all sound like drake.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="qa-block">
          <h3 class="qa-question">The Farmer was Replaced</h3>
          <div class="qa-answer">
            <p>As a person who works in software, web development, and tech as a general sector, there’s already a lot of AI in my world. I go into meetings where I hear about how we should make an agent, and that agent’s output can get fed to another agent. I see colleagues make AI posters, and take pictures of items to “ask Chat” what their dimensions are (spoken with the inflection one might use to describe a close and personal friend). We had a ruler.</p>
            <p>I’m not saying we have to wave sticks in the air and throw rocks at things. I understand the implications of what has been unleashed, and it is helpful to have some extra computational power for complex systems.</p>
            <p>There’s a game called The Long Walk I want to play sometime soon, and they used AI to debug a few LINES of their more troublesome code. People are calling it AI slop, and I disagree. That’s like the way this shit should be being used. I get that to some extent, there’s no going back.</p>
            <p>I don’t care if Claude makes your schedule. I don’t care if Bing Copilot fixes your python script. I don’t care if ChatGPT organizes your downloads folder.</p>
            <p>But I do have a sister who is an actress, a girlfriend who is an author and therapist, and many best friends who are drummers, PAs, choreographers, cinematographers, producers, screenplay writers, aspiring lawyers, and teachers…</p>
            <p>and AI should replace none of them. Argue with the wall.</p>
            <p><i>~Luca</i></p>
          </div>
        </div>

